[0.017339, "o", "\r\u001b[3d\u001b[37m\u001b[40mIn this lesson you will be given several excerpts from the classics.\r\u001b[4dTake your time and type them carefully.\r\u001b[35d\u001b(B\u001b[0;7m\u001b[37m\u001b[40m Press RETURN or SPACE to continue, ESC to return to the menu \u001b[35;99H Tutorial \u001b[35;63H\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[39;49m\u001b[37m\u001b[40m\u001b[110G"]
[107.721502, "o", "\r\u001b[2d\u001b[37m\u001b[40mA Christmas Carol, Stave One, Marley's Ghost\r\u001b[3d\u001b[K\u001b[4d\u001b(B\u001b[0;7m\u001b[37m\u001b[40mN\u001b(B\u001b[0m\u001b[37m\u001b[40mow, it is a fact, that there was nothing at all par-\r\u001b[5dticular about the knocker on the door, except that it\r\u001b[6dwas very large.  It is also a fact, that Scrooge had\r\u001b[7dseen it, night and morning, during his whole residence\r\u001b[8din that place; also that Scrooge has as little of what\r\u001b[9dis called fancy about him as any man in the city of\r\u001b[10dLondon, even including--which is a bold word--the\r\u001b[11dcorporation, aldermen, and livery.  Let it also be\r\u001b[12dborne in mind that Scrooge had not bestowed one\r\u001b[13dthought on Marley, since his last mention of his\r\u001b[14dseven year's dead partner that afternoon.  And then\r\u001b[15dlet any man explain to me, if he can, how it happened\r\u001b[16dthat Scrooge, having his key in the lock of the door,\r\u001b[17dsaw in the knocker, without its undergoing any inter-\r\u001b[18dmediate process of change--not a knocker, but Mar-\r\u001b[19dley's face.\u001b[35;98H\u001b[1K \u001b(B\u001b[0;7m\u001b[37m\u001b[40mSpeed test\r\u001b[4dN\u001b(B\u001b[m\u001b[39;49m\u001b[37m\u001b[40m\u001b[35;110H"]
